## Why

Neural organ-on-a-chip labs screen compounds by recording network activity for days at up to seven
concentrations in triplicate. Most of that budget goes into concentrations that turn out to be
uninformative, and the readout is analysed one endpoint at a time, with no uncertainty and no memory of the
other compounds already measured.

## What it does

1. **A chemical is a few-shot task.** One model, meta-learned across {{ trajectory_cv.json : n_chemicals }}
   chemicals of the EPA Network Formation Assay (NFA), forecasts the full dose x DIV 5, 7, 9 and 12
   trajectory of the 17 network features of a chemical it has never seen, from as few as one to four
   measured concentrations (NeuroTrajectory).
2. **An interpolation-informed gated decoder** with dose-local attention, which learns per feature and day
   how far to trust the measured neighbouring concentrations.
3. **A toxicology-specific trust layer:** cross-conformal intervals stratified by cytotoxicity and by the
   number of measured doses, with epistemic abstention on out-of-domain forecasts.
4. **A closed decision loop for the lab:** a hazard call, the next concentration to measure (DoseCompass) and
   a DIV 7 exit gate, each validated on real wells against exactly reproduced EPA rules, plus a directed
   chip layer fitted on axon-resolved recordings of a real four-compartment hippocampal MEA.

## How it works

![System overview](report/figures/fig1_system.png)

Public, licensed inputs are unified into a single frozen, provenance-tracked data asset (chemical x dose x
DIV x well x feature). NeuroTrajectory forecasts the dose x DIV x feature response of an unseen chemical from
k measured concentrations; calibration and abstention wrap every forecast; the decision layer turns that
forecast into a hazard call, a potency estimate and the next concentration to measure, closing the
laboratory loop. The chip layer is fitted separately on axon-resolved recordings of a four-compartment
hippocampal chip and is descriptive (no cross-platform calibration is claimed).

